Z||000006||terryk||07-10-2000||11:29 PM||||Only the 3.4L Camaro long block with the Fiero trimmings requires no substantial work.<P>The 3.4L DOHC V6 at 210HP bolts in but requires some subtantial work. You loose part of one hinge and some mods to one strut tower. Most of the work is wiring and exhaust.<P>The Buick 3800's don't just bolt in. You trade one set of problems for another over the DOHC. More wiring, exhaust and flywheel (manual) or axles (automatic). <P>The Northstar 4.0/4.6L V8 fits, but requires even more work. More wiring, exhaust and axles.<P>Then you get to the V8. You loose the right side of the car. The Fiero becomes a trike.<P>Then there's the Allision Aircraft V engine....<P>Add $250 for California. 8^)<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Philphine||07-11-2000||02:26 AM||||where dose the pre northstar caddy engine fit in that order?<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 07-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||07-11-2000||03:06 AM||||3.4L is 160hp<BR>3.4L Ram Air in new Grand Am's is advertised at 180hp||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||07-11-2000||08:01 AM||||Just a reminder that HP isn't everything.  The stock 3.4 has a lot more torque than the 140 hp 2.8. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan86gt3.8||07-11-2000||01:44 PM||||Don't forget the northstar requires alott of work done to the computer.  Lots of sensors and crazy nonsense have to be removed out of the computer, as far as I understand it.  Plus it has to be done in house at the 2 places that offer it.  But it would be one helluva fast fiero, If I had 7k in my hand right no I would probably calling up design one!<P>------------------<BR>86 gt fiero<BR>3.8/4speed auto<BR>ace conversion kit<BR>15" konig monsoon's<BR>rear swaybar/ and urethane on rear.<BR>Soon to come urethane all the way around. and new yellow paint w/ raised spoiler!!!!<BR>(also in way future years forced induction on my 3.8!!!!!!!!!)||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FieroGTRacingInc||07-11-2000||04:53 PM||||Is the 3.4l longblock,that is in the camaro alot different from the 3.4 DOHC?? Does 5the 3.4 dohc BOLT UP  as easy as the 3.4l Longblock???  thank's||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||07-11-2000||08:38 PM||||Thank you Carrolles! I'm always preaching to forget HP and go with torque, but at times it seems like nobody listens to me. I'm glad to hear someone else also believes in torque. The current 350 in my Batmobile probably don't put out much more than 200-210 HP. Lots of hot running 60 degree V6's out there with nearly that much HP. But, from the stoplight, that extra 100 ft/lbs of torque I have will leave you in the dust and put a big grin on my face.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||88formula||07-11-2000||09:31 PM||||The 3.4 Camaro motor in a fiero will put out more than the 160 HP. It's around 175 HP. The Camaro 3.4 will make more torque at 2000 RPM than a 2.8 can make in total and that is what makes the 3.4 swap in a fiero a fast fiero. A modified 3.4 can easily make more than 200HP and still have better torque than a 2.8. With a five speed the 3.4 pulls in third like the 2.8 pulls in second gear.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Black88GT||07-11-2000||10:02 PM||||I heard they under-rated the 3.4 in 1995 because they also offered the 3800 engine and they wanted more people to buy that over the 3.4.  Thats what I heard.  Ed Parks says they are 180 Hp too. Hopefully I can dyno mine when we are done and see what it is.<BR> <P>jeff||||reg||1
Z||000015||Phil||07-11-2000||10:05 PM||||I'm with Bat on this one.<BR> Take the torque which means go with a SC3800 over the N*. Had a chance to compare my 3800 with a N* on the dyno. The N* had me by about 14 HP but I had 18 more ft-lbs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
